// ATK-001's root cause: the spy planner used to take the first 20 planets by id, so in a populated
// universe the candidates were the accounts that played minutes ago and a later inactive farm was
// never reached. Selection is now by relevance in SQL before the cap, so the planted neighbour --
// created after a crowd of 25 -- is the body the planner chooses.
test('the spy planner reaches an inactive neighbour planted past the crowd', function (): void {
    $situation = Situation::of($this)
        ->resources(1_000_000, 1_000_000, 1_000_000)
        ->ships('espionage_probe', 5)
        ->crowd(25)
        ->inactiveNeighbour(daysQuiet: 8);

    $plan = app(QueueableSpyPlanner::class)->plan($this->currentUserId);
    $at = $situation->neighbour()?->getPlanetCoordinates();

    expect($plan)->not->toBeNull()
        ->and([$plan->targetGalaxy, $plan->targetSystem, $plan->targetPosition])->toBe([$at->galaxy, $at->system, $at->position]);
});
```

Note `$situation->neighbour()` returns ?PlanetService; if null, `$at->galaxy` errors. Better assert neighbour not null? It's planted so fine. But to be safe: `$at = $situation->neighbour()->getPlanetCoordinates();` — calling method on nullable triggers static analysis but runtime fine. Use `?->` and then in expect, PHP will error on null property. It's planted, fine.

Hmm, does plan() need resources? probes needed; ships('espionage_probe', 5) provided. Research for espionage? The planner doesn't check research. Fine.

Also `crowd(25)` creates foreign planets — but does createForeignPlanet create active users with time=now? Let me check to be sure the ordering test is meaningful. Actually not needed; the session test already proves it.

Wait, one concern: `activityIntelReader->activityAt($target)` for the neighbour — quiet() sets planet time_last_update to 8 days ago. Good.

And for crowd planets: created now → planet time_last_update now → activityAt true → skipped. So plan picks the neighbour. 

But: does `$plan` maybe target a crowd planet if activityAt returns false for them? createForeignPlanet's users may have time=0 (never recorded login) → sorting puts them last; activity based on planet row. Let me check ActivityIntelReader::activityAt to be sure. Actually the session test passes and flew Espionage — to whom? Unknown. The debug output showed plan=null after. Let me not over-assume. To be robust, instead of asserting exact coordinates, I could assert that the neighbour is chosen... that's the point. If activityAt for crowd planets is false, the planner might choose a crowd planet and my test fails. Let me check ActivityIntelReader and createForeignPlanet.

